### Component 3: Untrusted Input Isolation (`core/untrusted.py`)

Structural defence: retrieved content enters the model context as data only, never as instructions.

#### [NEW] `core/untrusted.py`
- `UntrustedContent` Pydantic model: `raw_text: str`, `source_id: str`, `source_version: str`, `sanitised: bool`.
- `wrap_retrieved(raw_text, source_id, source_version) -> UntrustedContent`: wraps any retrieved snippet.
- `format_for_context(items: list[UntrustedContent]) -> str`: renders content inside explicit `<DATA>...</DATA>` delimiters with a preamble warning the model this is untrusted data.
- `detect_injection_signals(text: str) -> list[str]`: heuristic-only logging layer (not a gate). Checks for patterns like "ignore previous instructions", role impersonation, tool-call JSON shapes, base64, etc. Returns a list of signal descriptions for audit logging. **Never blocks on its own.**
- The structural guarantee: `format_for_context` output is placed into a `user` message as a data block, never into a `system`/`developer` message. Tool-call-shaped text inside `<DATA>` blocks is inert because the LLM provider only parses tool calls from model output, not from user-message data blocks.

